  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 404. Shared by the marketing site, the documentation and the status page,
  // which is why it is a top-level neighbourhood rather than living under
  // `site.` or `docs.`: three surfaces render this sentence and it must be one
  // sentence. The status page reads these two keys straight out of
  // `PRODUCT_SOURCE`, because that app is English-only and has no translator.
  //
  // The digits are NOT here. `404` is a machine-readable code, which §5b puts
  // out of scope, and a key nobody could translate is a key a translator would
  // eventually translate.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The sign-in screen.
  //
  // THE LAST UNTRANSLATED SURFACE IN THE PRODUCT, and it was the one that most
  // needed translating. Every other screen in `apps/app` reads from this
  // catalogue; this one held its words as English literals, so a person who had
  // not yet signed in, and therefore had no stored preference, met the product
  // in a language they may not read. `locale-switcher.tsx` and
  // `api/locale/route.ts` were both written FOR this screen and each says so in
  // its own header. Neither was ever wired to it.
  //
  // THE ERROR SENTENCES ARE THE CAREFUL PART. Almost every one of them is a
  // refusal, and a refusal is the sentence shape CLAUDE.md 11b measures as the
  // one translation actually breaks: negation scope, obligation, and who is
  // acting on whom. "That domain cannot receive mail" must not become "you
  // cannot send mail to that domain", and "a reset lifts it" must not become an
  // instruction to reset something. Read the target, not a back-translation.
  //
  // NONE OF THEM NAMES A CAUSE THE PRODUCT CANNOT SEE. `check_email` says "if
  // that address can be used" rather than "we sent you an email", because
  // confirming that an address has an account turns this form into an existence
  // oracle. That hedge is load-bearing and must survive every translation.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
