var LEGAL_SOURCE = {
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 1. Privacy notice
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.privacy.title": "Privacy notice",
  "legal.privacy.lead": "What we collect, why, where it goes, and what you can make us do about it. Orvay is run by one person, and the public website collects one thing: an email address, if you choose to give us one.",
  "legal.privacy.controller.heading": "Who is responsible",
  // `{controller}` rather than the name, for the reason `CONTROLLER` exists at
  // all: the entity is named in four of the six documents and has to change in
  // one place. The pronoun in the next sentence is deliberately left in the
  // English string, because it is a fact about the current controller being a
  // natural person and a translator needs to see it to agree the sentence.
  "legal.privacy.controller.p1": "{controller} is the controller for the processing described here. He is a natural person and the sole operator of Orvay. There is no company. A Swiss Kollektivgesellschaft is intended and has not been formed, so there is no commercial register entry and no UID number.",
  "legal.privacy.controller.pair1.term": "Postal address",
  "legal.privacy.controller.pair2.term": "Email",
  "legal.privacy.controller.p2": "Two laws apply at the same time. The Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection (revFADP, in German revDSG), in force since 1 September 2023, applies because we are in Switzerland. Regulation (EU) 2016/679 (GDPR) applies because we offer a service to people in the European Union. Where the two differ, this notice states both.",
  "legal.privacy.controller.p3": "We hold no certification and we claim none. Not SOC 2, not ISO 27001, and no compliance badge of any kind. What follows is a description of what we actually do, written so that you can check it.",
  "legal.privacy.collected.heading": "What we collect",
  "legal.privacy.collected.p1": "The public website collects one item of personal data, and only if you hand it over: the email address you type into the waitlist form.",
  "legal.privacy.collected.p2": "Submitting that form writes a consent record. It holds your address in lower case, the exact wording of the consent sentence you were shown, the date and time, a label saying the address came from the waitlist form on our marketing site and has not been confirmed by a reply, and a SHA-256 digest of the address that we use to build your unsubscribe link. That is the whole record.",
  "legal.privacy.collected.p3": "We do not ask for your name. We set no analytics cookie, run no tracking pixel, and use no third party analytics service on the public website. We do not buy addresses, and we do not enrich yours from any other source.",
  "legal.privacy.collected.p4": "Cloudflare serves the website, and in doing so processes the technical details of every request, including your IP address. That happens whether or not you fill in the form, because it is how the page reaches you. Request logging is switched on, so those records are retained by Cloudflare under its own retention period.",
  "legal.privacy.collected.p5": "Signing in involves more, and this notice now describes it rather than promising to. Creating an account or signing in collects the email address you type, the password you choose (stored only as a hash, never as text, and never readable by us), the network address your browser connects from, and a record of the browser and device so that you can see and revoke your own sessions. Afterwards it covers the records your company creates.",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.heading": "Why we process it, and on what basis",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair1.term": "Writing to you when Orvay launches",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair1.detail": "Your consent. GDPR Art. 6(1)(a), and consent under the revFADP. You gave it by submitting the form under the sentence we showed you, and we store that sentence word for word so the basis can be checked rather than asserted.",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair2.term": "Keeping the record, including after you unsubscribe",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair2.detail": "Our obligation to be able to demonstrate consent, GDPR Art. 7(1) read with Art. 5(2). A record that we stopped is the only proof that we stopped.",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair3.term": "Serving the website and keeping it available and secure",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair3.detail": "Our legitimate interest in running a working website, GDPR Art. 6(1)(f). Under the revFADP this processing needs no separate justification, because it does not breach the principles the Act sets out.",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair4.term": "Signing you in, if you create an account",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair4.detail": "Performance of a contract with you, GDPR Art. 6(1)(b).",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair5.term": "Doing the work you ask an agent to do",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.pair5.detail": "Performance of a contract with you, GDPR Art. 6(1)(b). The text of your request is sent to a model vendor outside Switzerland and outside the EEA. See international transfers.",
  "legal.privacy.purposes.p1": "There is no other purpose. We do not profile visitors, we do not build advertising audiences, and we sell nothing to anyone.",
  "legal.privacy.automated.heading": "Automated decisions",
  "legal.privacy.automated.p1": "Nothing on the public website engages GDPR Art. 22. The sign-in screen is a different matter and this notice used to be silent about it, so here it is in full.",
  "legal.privacy.automated.p2": "Repeated failed sign-ins, sign-ups from throwaway addresses and similar patterns raise a score. Four things are scored separately and the strictest one wins: the account, the mailbox after provider aliases are folded together, the network address, and the block of addresses it sits in. Scores fade with time rather than resetting on a schedule. A high enough score slows sign-in down, then refuses new accounts, then leaves only password reset open, and at the top it refuses everything and ends existing sessions.",
  "legal.privacy.recipients.heading": "Who else sees it",
  "legal.privacy.recipients.p1": "We use service providers. Each one is named in the sub-processor list, with what it receives, where it processes and the safeguard the transfer rests on.",
  "legal.privacy.recipients.p2": "We disclose personal data to nobody else. If an authority compelled disclosure we would follow the law, and we would tell you unless we were forbidden from telling you.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.heading": "Where the data goes",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.p1": "Switzerland is not in the European Union and not in the European Economic Area. It is a third country holding an adequacy decision from the European Commission, and one from the United Kingdom. A transfer from the EEA or the UK to us therefore rests on adequacy and needs no further instrument.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair1.term": "Waitlist addresses, and every other database record",
  // `eu-central-2` is a provider region identifier and is left inside the
  // sentence rather than placeheld. It is not translatable, it is not
  // configurable from here, and a placeholder would suggest a renderer supplies
  // it when the honest answer is that changing region is a documented event.
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair1.detail": "Stored in PostgreSQL in Zurich, Switzerland, in a project whose region is eu-central-2. Safeguard: the EU adequacy decision for Switzerland.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair2.term": "Evidence artifacts",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair2.detail": "Stored in an object storage bucket pinned to the EU jurisdiction, so those objects stay on EU infrastructure. Safeguard: the jurisdiction setting, plus the standard contractual clauses in the provider agreement.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair3.term": "Generated tenant websites, and the page cache",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair3.detail": "Stored in buckets created with a European location hint. A hint is a preference and not a guarantee, so we do not describe these as jurisdiction bound. Safeguard: the standard contractual clauses in the provider agreement.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair4.term": "Handling the request itself",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair4.detail": "Our code runs at the network edge, worldwide, so the request that renders a page may execute close to you rather than in Europe. Safeguard: the standard contractual clauses in the provider agreement.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair5.term": "Model prompts",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.pair5.detail": "Text sent to Anthropic and to OpenAI is processed in the United States, outside Switzerland and outside the EEA. Safeguard: the standard contractual clauses in each vendor agreement. Your waitlist address is never part of that text.",
  "legal.privacy.transfers.p2": "Ask us which instrument a given provider relies on and we will send you what that provider publishes.",
  "legal.privacy.retention.heading": "How long we keep it",
  "legal.privacy.retention.p1": "The consent ledger is append only. Each record is chained to the one before it by a hash, so removing a row would destroy the proof that the remaining rows are unmodified. Unsubscribing therefore writes a revocation onto your record instead of erasing it.",
  "legal.privacy.retention.p2": "So the honest answer is this. We keep a waitlist record until you ask us to erase it, and there is no automatic expiry. No plan we sell carries a retention window, because nothing in the product enforces one yet. We would rather publish no number than a number nothing keeps.",
  "legal.privacy.retention.p3": "Erasure is handled by hand. There is no self-service delete button. Write to us, we will do it, and we will tell you when it is done.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.heading": "Your rights, and how to use them",
  // `30 days` is left as words rather than a `{days}` placeholder, unlike the
  // disclosure window in the security policy. The difference is that the
  // security window is a configured constant somebody may change, while this one
  // is the GDPR Art. 12(3) deadline: it is a fact about the law, so freezing it
  // into the sentence is correct and parameterising it would invite an edit that
  // makes the promise unlawful.
  "legal.privacy.rights.p1": "Every right below is exercised the same way. Email {email} from the address you want us to act on, or tell us which address it concerns. We answer within 30 days, and it costs nothing.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair1.term": "Access",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair1.detail": "Ask what we hold about you and we will send it. GDPR Art. 15, revFADP Art. 25.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair2.term": "Rectification",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair2.detail": "Tell us what is wrong and we will correct it. GDPR Art. 16, revFADP Art. 32.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair3.term": "Erasure",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair3.detail": "Ask us to delete your data and we will. GDPR Art. 17. Where the audit chain prevents removing a record, we destroy the personal data inside it and leave the remainder, which can still show that something happened and can no longer show what it said. The operator does this by hand.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair4.term": "Restriction",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair4.detail": "Ask us to stop processing while something is disputed and we will. GDPR Art. 18.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair5.term": "Portability",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair5.detail": "Ask for your data in a machine readable file and we will send it. GDPR Art. 20, revFADP Art. 28. This is a right, so it is free and it is available on every plan including the free one. We will never charge for it.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair6.term": "Objection",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair6.detail": "Object to processing we base on a legitimate interest, and we stop unless we can show grounds that override yours. GDPR Art. 21.",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair7.term": "Withdrawing consent",
  "legal.privacy.rights.pair7.detail": "Use the unsubscribe link in any message we send, or write to us. Withdrawal takes effect at once, and it does not make the processing before it unlawful. GDPR Art. 7(3).",
  "legal.privacy.rights.p2": "An unsubscribe writes a revocation into the consent ledger. It does not merely flip a switch in a mailing tool, because an unsubscribe that leaves the consent standing is a lie told twice.",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.heading": "Complaining about us",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.p1": "You can complain to a supervisory authority, and you do not have to talk to us first. There are separate routes, and you may use whichever applies to you.",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair1.term": "Switzerland",
  // The FDPIC's postal address is structure sitting inside a sentence, and it is
  // NOT placeheld: it belongs to a regulator rather than to us, so there is no
  // record for a renderer to read it from and nothing that would keep a
  // placeholder in sync. The translation instruction is the ordinary one for a
  // foreign address, which is to leave it alone. `in German EDOEB` will read
  // oddly in the German catalogue and the German translator should recast that
  // parenthetical rather than transliterate it.
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair1.detail": "Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner (FDPIC, in German EDOEB), Feldeggweg 1, 3003 Bern, Switzerland.",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair2.term": "European Union",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair2.detail": "Your local supervisory authority: the one where you live, the one where you work, or the one where the problem happened. Each member state names its own, and any of the three may take your complaint.",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair3.term": "United Kingdom",
  "legal.privacy.complaints.pair3.detail": "The Information Commissioner's Office.",
  "legal.privacy.eu-representative.heading": "Our EU representative, which we do not have yet",
  "legal.privacy.eu-representative.p1": "GDPR Art. 27 requires a controller established outside the European Union that offers services to people inside it to appoint a representative in the Union. We are established in Switzerland, we offer a waitlist to people in the Union, and we have not appointed one. We are arranging it.",
  "legal.privacy.eu-representative.p2": "That sentence is on this page rather than left out. A privacy notice that says nothing about Art. 27 reads as though the duty did not exist. Until the appointment is made, writing to the address at the top of this page reaches the controller directly.",
  "legal.privacy.eu-representative.p3": "The mirror duty does not apply to us. Art. 14 revFADP requires a representative in Switzerland only from controllers domiciled abroad, and we are domiciled here.",
  "legal.privacy.required.heading": "Whether you have to give us anything",
  "legal.privacy.required.p1": "On the public website, no. The waitlist form is the only place that asks for any, and the only consequence of leaving it empty is that we will not write to you when Orvay launches.",
  "legal.privacy.security.heading": "How it is protected",
  "legal.privacy.security.item1": "The database is in Zurich, and the operator is the only person with credentials for it.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item2": "Tenant data is isolated in the database by RESTRICTIVE row level security, so a query cannot see the rows of another company even if the application asks for them.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item3": "The tenant identity is set inside the transaction and never on the connection, so a pooled connection cannot carry the scope of one tenant into the query of the next.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item4": "The audit trail is append only and hash chained. A changed record is detectable rather than merely discouraged.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item5": "The consent ledger grants the application no delete, and the stored wording of a consent cannot be rewritten.",
  // `httpOnly` and `Secure` are cookie attribute names from RFC 6265, not
  // English words that happen to be capitalised. They are inside the message
  // because pulling two identifiers out of a nine-word sentence would leave a
  // fragment nobody can translate, and the instruction to a translator is the
  // one that applies to any code identifier in prose: copy it exactly.
  "legal.privacy.security.item6": "Session cookies are httpOnly and Secure.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item7": "Secrets are held as deployment secrets. None of them are in the source code.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item8": "The public marketing site holds no database binding at all, so no marketing page has a path to tenant data.",
  "legal.privacy.security.item9": "Our providers encrypt data at rest and in transit as part of their own service.",
  "legal.privacy.security.p1": "We claim nothing beyond that. There is no penetration test report, no certification, and one person with access.",
  "legal.privacy.changes.heading": "Changes to this notice",
  "legal.privacy.changes.p1": "The date at the top is when this text last changed. If a change affects what we do with data you have already given us, we will write to you at the address we hold before it takes effect. Otherwise the new version simply replaces this one.",
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2. Terms of service
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.terms.title": "Terms of service",
  "legal.terms.lead": "The agreement between you and the person who runs Orvay. It is short because the product is pre-launch and there is not much to agree about yet.",
  "legal.terms.parties.heading": "Who this is with",
  "legal.terms.parties.p1": "This agreement is between you and {controller}, a natural person operating Orvay from {address}. There is no company. A Swiss Kollektivgesellschaft is intended and has not been formed. If it is formed, this agreement transfers to it, and we will tell you before that happens.",
  "legal.terms.parties.p2": "Using our websites, joining the waitlist, or using the product means you accept these terms. If you do not accept them, do not use the service.",
  "legal.terms.service.heading": "What the service is",
  "legal.terms.service.p1": "Orvay is an operating system for running a company with AI agents. A company gives it goals, context, integrations, permissions and budgets. Orvay proposes work, gets it approved where approval is required, executes it, has the result verified by something other than the thing that did the work, and keeps an auditable record.",
  "legal.terms.service.p2": "It is not a chatbot and it is not a coding assistant. An agent saying it finished is not proof that anything is finished, and the product exists because of that sentence.",
  "legal.terms.prelaunch.heading": "It is pre-launch, and the waitlist is not a purchase",
  "legal.terms.prelaunch.p1": "Orvay is not generally available. Joining the waitlist buys nothing, reserves nothing, and creates no entitlement to a place, a price or a launch date. No payment is taken. We may change the product, the plans and the prices before launch, and we may decide not to launch at all.",
  "legal.terms.prelaunch.p2": "Anything shown as simulated did not touch an external system. We label it on the artifact itself, because a demonstration presented as a real run is a lie whatever the disclaimer at the bottom of the page says.",
  "legal.terms.account.heading": "Your account",
  "legal.terms.account.p1": "You are responsible for the security of your account and for everything done through it. Tell us as soon as you think somebody else has access. You must be old enough to enter into a contract where you live.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.heading": "What you may not do",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item1": "Break the law, or use Orvay to help somebody else break it.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item2": "Send cold outreach, spam, or any message to a person who has given no basis for being contacted. The product refuses this at the consent gate, and doing it by another route is still a breach of these terms.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item3": "Attack the service, probe it for weaknesses without asking us first, or try to reach data belonging to another customer.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item4": "Resell the service, sublicense it, or run it for a third party without a written agreement with us.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item5": "Try to make an agent take an action you could not lawfully take yourself.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item6": "Upload personal data you have no lawful basis to hold.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.item7": "Run a business we will not work for: sexual content or sexual services, anything that sexualises a child, malware and intrusion tooling, fraud, the sale of weapons or controlled substances, or targeted harassment of a person. This is a refusal about the work, not a judgement about you, and it applies whatever the law where you are says.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.p1": "We may suspend an account that is doing any of this. Where we can, we will tell you first and give you a chance to put it right. Where the harm is immediate we will suspend first and explain afterwards.",
  "legal.terms.acceptable-use.p2": "Two checks enforce the line above, and it is worth knowing exactly what they are so you do not mistake them for more. The name and the web address you give us are checked against a short list of terms when you set up, before we fetch anything. Separately, an agent refuses to work on a goal that asks for one of these things. Neither is a review of everything you do, neither reads your data, and neither is a substitute for you knowing what you are running.",
  "legal.terms.your-instructions.heading": "What your agents do is your responsibility",
  "legal.terms.your-instructions.p1": "You decide what your agents may do. You set the goals, grant the capabilities, approve the actions that need approval, and set the budget. An action taken inside the authority you granted is your action, not ours.",
  "legal.terms.your-instructions.p2": "This is not a disclaimer bolted on afterwards. The product is built so that the authority is explicit and the record shows who granted it. Read what you approve, because the approval record is the evidence that you did.",
  "legal.terms.your-instructions.p3": "Some things are refused by the product and cannot be granted at all. Outbound telephone calls placed by an agent are one of them, permanently.",
  "legal.terms.ip.heading": "Who owns what",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair1.term": "Our software",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair1.detail": "The Orvay software, the design system, the documentation and the brand stay ours. Using the service gives you a limited, revocable, non-exclusive right to use it while your account is active, and nothing more.",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair2.term": "Your content",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair2.detail": "Everything you put in stays yours: your goals, your documents, your contacts, your policies. We claim no ownership of it, and we do not use it to train models.",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair3.term": "Work product",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair3.detail": "What the product makes for you, including generated websites, copy and code, is yours. We claim no ownership of the output.",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair4.term": "Feedback",
  "legal.terms.ip.pair4.detail": "If you send us an idea for the product we may use it without owing you anything. Do not send us anything confidential as feedback.",
  "legal.terms.ip.p1": "Exporting your personal data is a right, so it is free on every plan including the free one. Exporting a generated website is a product feature, and it is part of what a paid plan buys. We keep those two apart deliberately, and we say which is which on the pricing page rather than after you have paid.",
  "legal.terms.availability.heading": "Availability",
  "legal.terms.availability.p1": "We promise no uptime. There is no service level agreement, no guaranteed support response time, and one person operating the service. When we can offer those, we will write them down and charge for them.",
  "legal.terms.availability.p2": "We may change or withdraw features. If a change removes something you rely on, we will give you notice and, where the change is material, a way out.",
  "legal.terms.money.heading": "Money",
  "legal.terms.money.p1": "Nothing is charged today. When plans go on sale, the price, what it includes and every limit will be stated on the pricing page before you pay, not after. Quota is measured in what your usage actually costs us, and credits are how that is displayed. The free plan stops when its allowance is gone and never runs up a bill.",
  "legal.terms.warranty.heading": "What we do not promise",
  "legal.terms.warranty.p1": "The service is provided as it is. So far as Swiss law permits, we give no warranty that it will be uninterrupted or error free, that it is fit for a particular purpose, or that any output is correct.",
  "legal.terms.warranty.p2": "Verification is an independence mechanism, not an oracle. A verified result means a second actor checked the first one and the evidence was recorded. It does not mean the outcome is guaranteed correct, and we do not sell it as one.",
  "legal.terms.liability.heading": "Liability, honestly",
  "legal.terms.liability.p1": "Swiss law does not allow us to exclude liability for unlawful intent or for gross negligence in advance. Art. 100(1) of the Swiss Code of Obligations makes such a clause void. We have not written one.",
  "legal.terms.liability.p2": "For slight negligence, our liability is limited to what you paid us in the twelve months before the event, and we are not liable for indirect or consequential loss, for lost profit, or for lost data beyond what we can restore from our own archive. That amount is what you have actually paid, and it is no longer nil for everyone: plans and credit packs are on sale.",
  "legal.terms.liability.p3": "Nothing here limits liability for death or personal injury, or any other liability that cannot be limited under the law that applies to you. If you are a consumer, the mandatory consumer protection of your own country still applies, and these terms do not take it away.",
  "legal.terms.termination.heading": "Ending it",
  "legal.terms.termination.p1": "You can stop using the service at any time and ask us to close your account. We will export your data on request first.",
  "legal.terms.termination.p2": "We can end this agreement if you breach these terms and do not put it right within a reasonable time after we ask, or immediately where the breach is serious. We can also end it on 30 days notice if we stop offering the service.",
  "legal.terms.termination.p3": "When it ends we return or delete your data on request. The audit chain that proves what happened cannot be deleted without destroying that proof, so the personal data inside it is destroyed instead and the remainder stays.",
  "legal.terms.changes.heading": "Changing these terms",
  "legal.terms.changes.p1": "We can change these terms, and the date at the top says when they last changed. For a change that materially affects you we will give at least 30 days notice by email to the address on your account, or on the website if you have no account. Continuing to use the service after the change takes effect means you accept it. If you do not accept it, stop using the service and ask us to close your account.",
  "legal.terms.law.heading": "Law and forum",
  "legal.terms.law.p1": "Swiss law applies, without its conflict of law rules and without the United Nations Convention on Contracts for the International Sale of Goods.",
  "legal.terms.law.p2": "The courts of Basel, Switzerland have jurisdiction. If you are a consumer this does not remove your right to bring a claim in the courts where you live, or the protection of the mandatory law there. The Lugano Convention gives you that, and we are not trying to contract around it.",
  "legal.terms.law.p3": "If a clause here turns out to be invalid, the rest stays in force.",
  "legal.terms.contact.heading": "Contact",
  "legal.terms.contact.pair1.term": "Email",
  "legal.terms.contact.pair2.term": "Post",
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3. Imprint
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.imprint.title": "Imprint",
  "legal.imprint.lead": "Who runs this website, and where to reach them.",
  "legal.imprint.provider.heading": "Provider",
  "legal.imprint.provider.pair1.term": "Responsible person",
  "legal.imprint.provider.pair2.term": "Legal form",
  // This detail is `CONTROLLER.form`, so it is the one string in that record
  // which is PROSE rather than an identifier: two sentences a reader parses,
  // not an address a postal service parses. It is keyed here for that reason,
  // and whoever wires the renderer must take it from the catalogue rather than
  // from `CONTROLLER`, or the German imprint will state the legal form in
  // English. The same applies to `legal.imprint.register.p1` below.
  "legal.imprint.provider.pair2.detail": "A natural person, and the sole operator of Orvay. There is no company.",
  "legal.imprint.provider.pair3.term": "Address",
  "legal.imprint.provider.pair4.term": "Email",
  "legal.imprint.provider.p1": "Art. 3(1)(s) of the Swiss Federal Act against Unfair Competition requires anyone offering something over the internet to give a clear statement of identity and a contact address. This page is that statement.",
  "legal.imprint.register.heading": "Commercial register",
  "legal.imprint.register.p1": "Not entered in any commercial register. A Swiss Kollektivgesellschaft (general partnership) is intended. It has not been formed, so there is no UID and no CHE number to give.",
  "legal.imprint.register.p2": "This page changes on the day that changes. Until then, the person named above is personally the provider of these sites.",
  "legal.imprint.vat.heading": "VAT",
  "legal.imprint.vat.p1": "Not registered for Swiss VAT. There is no UID, so there is no VAT number. If that changes, the number appears here.",
  "legal.imprint.editorial.heading": "Responsibility for the content",
  "legal.imprint.editorial.p1": "{controller} is responsible for the content of these sites, at the address above.",
  "legal.imprint.editorial.p2": "Anything shown as simulated is a demonstration and did not touch an external system. We label it on the artifact itself rather than relying on a note at the bottom of a page.",
  "legal.imprint.links.heading": "Links",
  "legal.imprint.links.p1": "Where we link to a site we do not run, we do not control what it says and we take no responsibility for it. Tell us if a link is broken or points somewhere it should not.",
  "legal.imprint.disputes.heading": "Dispute resolution",
  "legal.imprint.disputes.p1": "We are not obliged to take part in proceedings before a consumer arbitration board, and we do not do so. Write to the address above and a person reads it.",
  "legal.imprint.disputes.p2": "We do not link to the online dispute resolution platform of the European Commission, and we make no claim about whether it is available. That platform was created for traders established in the European Union. We are established in Switzerland.",
  "legal.imprint.security.heading": "Reporting a security problem",
  "legal.imprint.security.p1": "Write to {email}. The security contact page says what to include and what happens next.",
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4. Sub-processor list
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.subprocessors.title": "Sub-processors",
  "legal.subprocessors.lead": "Every third party that processes data for us: what it gets, where it processes, and whether it is receiving anything today.",
  /**
   * The three status labels, keyed by the `SubProcessorStatus` member rather
   * than by the pair position that renders them.
   *
   * This is the one place the key scheme takes a non-section id in the section
   * slot, and the reason is reuse: the label appears once as a pair term in
   * `how-to-read` and again on every entry in the list, so a position-derived
   * key would either be duplicated or would tie the list rendering to a clause
   * it has nothing to do with. Keying by the union member also means adding a
   * fourth status is a compile error in every catalogue, which is the same
   * property the rest of this file is built on. `status` cannot collide with a
   * section id, because the sub-processor notice's sections are `how-to-read`,
   * `list` and `changes`.
   */
  "legal.subprocessors.status.in_use": "In use today",
  "legal.subprocessors.status.configured": "Wired, and receiving only where its credential is set",
  "legal.subprocessors.status.not_engaged": "Named in the product, connected to nothing",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.heading": "How to read this",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.p1": "This list is derived from the deployment configuration rather than from memory: the bindings each Worker holds, the environment variables it may carry, and the vendor libraries in the codebase. Each entry carries one of three states.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair1.detail": "Data is flowing to this provider now.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair2.detail": "The wiring is real, and whether anything reaches the provider depends on a credential set for that deployment. We list it because it may be receiving data.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair3.detail": "The name appears in the product and is connected to nothing. It receives no data at all.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.p2": "We keep the third state rather than deleting those rows. A vendor name that a customer can see in the product and cannot find on this list is what makes a sub-processor list untrustworthy.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.p3": "Each provider uses its own sub-processors, for example the cloud infrastructure underneath a managed database. Each of them publishes its own list.",
  "legal.subprocessors.list.heading": "The list",
  "legal.subprocessors.changes.heading": "Changing the list",
  "legal.subprocessors.changes.p1": "We give customers at least 30 days notice by email before a new sub-processor starts processing their personal data, and you may object in writing during that period on reasonable data protection grounds. If we cannot resolve the objection you may terminate the affected service without penalty. That commitment is part of the data processing agreement, not a courtesy.",
  /**
   * The entries themselves, keyed by `SubProcessor.id` in the section slot.
   *
   * These are customer-visible legal text: the sub-processor list is what a
   * controller reads before authorising us under Art. 28(2), so "where it
   * processes" and "what makes the status true" have to be readable in the
   * reader's language for the disclosure to do its job at all. Only `name` and
   * `id` stay behind as structure, because a vendor's trading name is the same
   * word in every jurisdiction and translating it would make the entry
   * uncheckable against that vendor's own published terms.
   *
   * `dataN`, `locationN`, `safeguard` and `statusDetail` map one-to-one onto
   * `dataCategories`, `processingLocations`, `transferSafeguard` and
   * `statusDetail`. The array fields are numbered from 1 in source order, with
   * the same caveat as `itemN`: an inserted category renumbers the ones after
   * it and that is a translation event.
   */
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.service": "Serves every request. Workers compute, R2 object storage, Hyperdrive database pooling, Durable Objects, Workflows, DNS, request logging, and the email route that sends operator alerts.",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data1": "Request metadata for every visit, including IP address, user agent and the page requested",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data2": "A waitlist submission while it is in transit to the database",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data3": "Evidence artifacts and event log archives at rest",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data4": "Generated tenant websites and the rendered page cache",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data5": "Operator alert emails, which go to one address belonging to the operator",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.location1": "Workers execute at the edge, worldwide, so the code that renders a page may run close to you rather than in Europe",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.location2": "The evidence bucket is pinned to the EU jurisdiction, so those objects stay on EU infrastructure",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.location3": "The tenant site bucket and the page cache bucket carry a European location hint. A hint is a preference and not a jurisdiction guarantee, and we do not present them as one",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.location4": "Durable Object placement is fixed when the object is created and is not chosen by us",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.safeguard": "Cloudflare is established in the United States. The instrument is its data processing addendum with the standard contractual clauses.",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.statusDetail": "Every Worker in the product runs here. Request logging is switched on, so Cloudflare retains request records under its own retention period.",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.service": "Managed PostgreSQL, and Supabase Auth used only as an identity provider. The auto-generated data API is switched off and the domain tables sit in a schema it does not expose.",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.data1": "Waitlist email addresses and their consent records",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.data2": "Company, membership, contract, approval, evidence and audit records once the product is in use",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.data3": "Your sign-in identity, if you create an account",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.location1": "Project region eu-central-2, which is Zurich, Switzerland",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.safeguard": "A transfer from the EEA to Switzerland rests on the European Commission adequacy decision for Switzerland and needs no further instrument. Supabase is established in the United States, and its own access is covered by its data processing addendum with the standard contractual clauses.",
  "legal.subprocessors.supabase.statusDetail": "Holds the consent ledger and every domain table. The earlier project in Frankfurt, region eu-central-1, is being retired in favour of the Zurich one.",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.service": "Model inference. Claude is the default for most task classes, including the one that writes a generated website.",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.data1": "The text of the request you make to an agent, and the context assembled for it",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.data2": "No waitlist address is ever part of that text",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.location1": "United States. Outside Switzerland and outside the EEA",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.safeguard": "The standard contractual clauses under the vendor data processing addendum. We rely on the clauses rather than on a framework certification.",
  "legal.subprocessors.anthropic.statusDetail": "The website generation Worker calls a model when it has a vendor key. It is the only Worker in the product that invokes a model at all.",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.service": "Model inference, used where the routing table sends a task class to an OpenAI model. It is the second vendor, which is what lets a verification be run by a different vendor from the one that did the work.",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.data1": "The text of the request you make to an agent, and the context assembled for it",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.data2": "No waitlist address is ever part of that text",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.location1": "United States. Outside Switzerland and outside the EEA",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.safeguard": "The standard contractual clauses under the vendor data processing addendum. We rely on the clauses rather than on a framework certification.",
  "legal.subprocessors.openai.statusDetail": "The website generation Worker routes its code writing task class to an OpenAI model, and reaches it when it has a vendor key.",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.service": "Error and incident reporting, read by the operator.",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.data1": "The one line summary and the detail of an operational alert",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.data2": "Its severity and kind, and the identifier of the company it relates to where there is one",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.data3": "No waitlist address, no account identity, and no customer content",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.location1": "The European region, which is where the project was created. Sentry is established in the United States",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.safeguard": "Sentry's data processing addendum with the standard contractual clauses.",
  "legal.subprocessors.sentry.statusDetail": "The alerting Worker builds a Sentry channel only when a reporting endpoint is configured on that deployment. With none configured the channel is absent and nothing is sent.",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.service": "Payment processing and tax calculation.",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.data1": "Your billing contact details and the plan or credit pack you chose",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.data2": "Card details, which you type on a page Stripe serves on its own domain and which never reach us",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.location1": "Stripe processes in the United States and in Ireland",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.safeguard": "Stripe is established in the United States. Transfers rest on the standard contractual clauses in its data processing agreement, and on the Swiss addendum to them.",
  "legal.subprocessors.stripe.statusDetail": 'This entry said the opposite until 20 August 2026, and the correction matters more than the wording. It read "there is no Stripe library in the codebase and no Stripe credential in any deployment" and "nothing reaches Stripe" while the product could already take a card. That was written when it was true and left standing when it stopped being true, which is the failure this whole page exists to avoid. What is true now: checkout hands your browser to a page Stripe serves, the card is typed there and nowhere else, and this product has no field for a card number at any point.',
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 5. Data processing agreement (GDPR Art. 28)
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.dpa.title": "Data processing agreement",
  "legal.dpa.lead": "The terms on which we process personal data on your behalf, under Art. 28 GDPR. It takes effect when you become a customer. Nobody is a customer today, so nothing is being processed under it yet.",
  "legal.dpa.roles.heading": "Roles",
  "legal.dpa.roles.p1": "You are the controller. We are the processor. This agreement covers the personal data we process on your behalf when you use Orvay.",
  "legal.dpa.roles.p2": "It does not cover personal data we process for our own purposes, such as your billing contact details or a waitlist address. For those we are the controller, and the privacy notice applies instead.",
  "legal.dpa.roles.p3": "It forms part of the terms of service. Where the two conflict on a data protection question, this agreement wins.",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.heading": "Subject matter, duration, nature and purpose",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair1.term": "Subject matter",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair1.detail": "Processing the personal data contained in the goals, contexts, documents, contacts, policies and records you put into Orvay, so that the service can run.",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair2.term": "Duration",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair2.detail": "For as long as your account exists, and after that only for as long as it takes to return or destroy the data.",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair3.term": "Nature and purpose",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair3.detail": "Storage, retrieval, transmission to the sub-processors on the published list, and submission to model vendors where you instruct an agent to do work that needs one.",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair4.term": "Categories of data subject",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair4.detail": "Your staff, your contacts, and anyone else whose data you choose to put in.",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair5.term": "Categories of personal data",
  "legal.dpa.subject-matter.pair5.detail": "Identity and contact details, role and employment information, the content of communications you record, and whatever else you upload. Do not put special category data under Art. 9 GDPR into Orvay. We are not built for it and we do not accept it.",
  "legal.dpa.instructions.heading": "Processing only on your instructions",
  "legal.dpa.instructions.p1": "We process personal data only on your documented instructions, including for a transfer to a third country, unless a law we are subject to requires otherwise. If such a law requires it, we tell you before processing unless that law forbids the notification. Art. 28(3)(a) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.instructions.p2": "Your instructions are this agreement, the terms of service, and what you do in the product. Configuring a company, granting a capability, approving a contract and running an action are all instructions, and each one leaves a record.",
  "legal.dpa.instructions.p3": "If we consider an instruction to breach the GDPR or the revFADP, we tell you immediately and may pause that processing until it is resolved.",
  "legal.dpa.confidentiality.heading": "Confidentiality",
  "legal.dpa.confidentiality.p1": "Everyone we authorise to process your personal data is bound to confidentiality. Art. 28(3)(b) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.confidentiality.p2": "Today that is one person, the operator, bound by law and by this agreement. There are no employees. Before anyone else is authorised they will be under a written confidentiality undertaking, and this clause will say so.",
  "legal.dpa.security.heading": "Security measures",
  "legal.dpa.security.p1": "The measures below are the ones we actually have, appropriate to the risk under Art. 32 GDPR. We hold no certificate and claim none.",
  // These eleven overlap heavily with `legal.privacy.security.item*` and are
  // keyed separately on purpose. The privacy list is a description offered to a
  // visitor; this one is a contractual Art. 32 representation to a controller.
  // A shared key would mean a future edit to one document silently amending the
  // other, which is exactly the failure a clause id is supposed to prevent.
  "legal.dpa.security.item1": "The database is hosted in Zurich, Switzerland. Evidence artifacts are held in object storage pinned to the EU jurisdiction.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item2": "Tenant isolation is enforced in the database by RESTRICTIVE row level security, so a query cannot see the rows of another company even when the application asks for them.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item3": "The tenant identity is set inside the transaction, never on the connection, so a pooled connection cannot carry the scope of one tenant into the transaction of the next.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item4": "The audit trail is append only and hash chained. Editing or removing a record breaks the chain and is detectable.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item5": "The consent ledger grants the application no delete, and the stored wording of a consent cannot be rewritten.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item6": "Everything that reaches the outside world passes one admission function with eight gates in a fixed order. A request for the wrong tenant is answered as though the resource did not exist, so it cannot be used to find out what exists.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item7": "An action with a legal or similarly significant effect on a person requires a recorded human approval. The approval is stored as a scoped, bounded record rather than a flag, so it can be produced as evidence.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item8": "Erasure is designed as destruction of a per subject encryption key rather than deletion of a row, so the audit chain survives and the content does not. The key store exists. Applying it to every field is not finished, and until it is, erasure is carried out by hand.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item9": "Session cookies are httpOnly and Secure. Secrets are deployment secrets and are not in the source code.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item10": "The public marketing site holds no database binding, so no marketing page has a path to tenant data.",
  "legal.dpa.security.item11": "Our providers encrypt data at rest and in transit as part of their own service.",
  "legal.dpa.security.p2": "What we do not have: a penetration test report, a certification, a security team, or round the clock monitoring. One person operates the service. We would rather you knew that now than found it out later.",
  "legal.dpa.subprocessors.heading": "Sub-processors",
  "legal.dpa.subprocessors.p1": "You give general authorisation for us to engage sub-processors, under Art. 28(2) and 28(3)(d) GDPR. The current list is published, and it names each one, what it receives, where it processes and the safeguard the transfer rests on.",
  "legal.dpa.subprocessors.p2": "We give you at least 30 days notice by email before a new sub-processor starts processing your personal data. You may object in writing within that period on reasonable data protection grounds. If we cannot resolve your objection you may terminate the affected part of the service without penalty, and we refund any prepaid fee for the unused period.",
  "legal.dpa.subprocessors.p3": "Each sub-processor is bound by a written contract with data protection obligations no weaker than these. Where one fails to meet them, we remain fully liable to you for its performance. Art. 28(4) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.transfers.heading": "International transfers",
  "legal.dpa.transfers.p1": "Where a sub-processor is outside Switzerland and the European Economic Area, the transfer relies on the safeguard named for it in the sub-processor list. For the model vendors the instrument is the standard contractual clauses. We do not rely on a framework certification.",
  "legal.dpa.transfers.p2": "Switzerland holds an adequacy decision from the European Commission, so a transfer from the EEA to our database in Zurich needs no further instrument.",
  "legal.dpa.data-subject-requests.heading": "Helping you answer data subjects",
  "legal.dpa.data-subject-requests.p1": "We help you meet a data subject request, taking into account the nature of the processing. Art. 28(3)(e) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.data-subject-requests.p2": "Where the product can answer it, you can answer it yourself. Your contacts, uploads, consent records and audit trail are exportable in a machine readable format on every plan, including the free one, because portability is a right and not a feature.",
  "legal.dpa.data-subject-requests.p3": "If a data subject contacts us directly about data we hold for you, we do not answer for you. We tell you promptly and pass it on.",
  "legal.dpa.assistance.heading": "Helping you with Arts. 32 to 36",
  "legal.dpa.assistance.p1": "We give you reasonable help with security of processing (Art. 32), notification of a breach to the authority and to data subjects (Arts. 33 and 34), data protection impact assessments (Art. 35) and prior consultation (Art. 36), taking into account the nature of the processing and what is available to us. Art. 28(3)(f) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.assistance.p2": "The audit trail is the main thing we can hand you, and it was designed to be evidence rather than a log.",
  "legal.dpa.breach.heading": "Breach notification",
  // The two deadlines here are contractual and statutory respectively, and
  // neither is a placeholder. 48 hours is a promise this document makes and 72
  // hours is Art. 33(1); a renderer that could vary either would be able to
  // amend the agreement, which is not a rendering concern.
  "legal.dpa.breach.p1": "We notify you without undue delay after becoming aware of a personal data breach affecting your data, and in any case within 48 hours. Art. 33(2) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.breach.p2": "The notification describes what happened, the categories and approximate number of people and records affected, the likely consequences, the measures taken or proposed, and a contact point. Where we do not know all of it at once we send what we have and follow up.",
  "legal.dpa.breach.p3": "Notifying your own supervisory authority within your 72 hour deadline remains yours to do. We do not notify on your behalf.",
  "legal.dpa.deletion.heading": "Return or deletion at the end",
  "legal.dpa.deletion.p1": "When the service ends you choose: we return your personal data in a machine readable export, or we delete it. Art. 28(3)(g) GDPR. We act within 30 days of your instruction. If you give no instruction within 30 days of the end, we delete.",
  "legal.dpa.deletion.p2": "The audit chain cannot be deleted without destroying the proof that the rest of it is unmodified. For those records we destroy the personal data inside them and keep the remainder, which can still show that something happened and when, and can no longer show what it said.",
  "legal.dpa.deletion.p3": "Where a law requires us to keep something, we keep only that, and we tell you what it is and why.",
  "legal.dpa.audits.heading": "Audits and information",
  "legal.dpa.audits.p1": "We make available to you the information you reasonably need to demonstrate that we meet these obligations, and we allow and contribute to an audit or inspection you conduct or mandate. Art. 28(3)(h) GDPR.",
  "legal.dpa.audits.p2": "In practice: ask, and we answer in writing, show you the configuration, and give you the audit trail for your own company. There is no third party audit report to send you, because we hold no certification and we will not imply otherwise.",
  "legal.dpa.audits.p3": "An on-site inspection is arranged by agreement, once a year, unless a breach or a regulator makes another one necessary. You carry your own cost.",
  "legal.dpa.liability-term.heading": "Liability and term",
  "legal.dpa.liability-term.p1": "This agreement lasts as long as we process personal data for you. Liability under it is governed by the terms of service, and nothing in it limits a liability the GDPR places on us directly under Art. 82.",
  "legal.dpa.law.heading": "Law",
  "legal.dpa.law.p1": "Swiss law applies and the courts of Basel have jurisdiction, on the same terms and with the same consumer carve out as the terms of service. Where the GDPR applies to a processing operation, it applies to it regardless of this clause.",
  "legal.dpa.signing.heading": "How to enter into it",
  "legal.dpa.signing.p1": "This agreement is part of the terms you accept when you create an account, and we send a countersigned copy on request. It said there was nothing to sign because there were no customers, which stopped being true when plans went on sale.",
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // 6. Security contact
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.security.title": "Security contact",
  "legal.security.lead": "How to report a vulnerability, and what happens after you do.",
  "legal.security.report.heading": "Reporting",
  "legal.security.report.pair1.term": "Email",
  "legal.security.report.pair2.term": "Preferred languages",
  // The human-readable twin of `SECURITY_CONTACT.preferredLanguages`, which is
  // `['en', 'de']`. The tags are structure and stay in `legal.ts`; this sentence
  // is what a person reads and is translated. They can disagree, and a test that
  // asserts the pair still agrees would be worth having, which is a note for
  // whoever owns the renderer rather than something this file can enforce.
  "legal.security.report.pair2.detail": "English, then German.",
  // `{ackHours}` is a NUMBER, and "hours" is written here rather than carried in
  // with it. It was `{ackWindow}`, interpolating the string `'72 hours'`, which
  // meant the placeholder smuggled English into every translation: the German
  // page rendered "innerhalb von 72 Stunden" only if a translator guessed, and
  // "innerhalb von 72 hours" otherwise. Extracting the commitment as a
  // placeholder was always right, since the number is a promise somebody may
  // change and must not be frozen into six catalogues. Extracting the UNIT with
  // it was the defect. A quantity is structure; its unit word is prose.
  "legal.security.report.p1": "Send what you found, how to reproduce it, and what you think the impact is. One person reads that address and will acknowledge within {ackHours} hours.",
  "legal.security.report.p2": "Please do not test against data belonging to other people. If a proof needs a second account, ask and we will make one for you.",
  "legal.security.scope.heading": "Scope",
  "legal.security.scope.p1": "The sites below, and the product behind them. Anything hosted by a provider on our behalf is theirs to receive, so report it to them and tell us as well.",
  "legal.security.no-bounty.heading": "No bug bounty",
  "legal.security.no-bounty.p1": "We do not pay for reports. This is one person before launch with no revenue, and pretending otherwise would waste your time. We will credit you if you want the credit, and we will tell you what we did about the report.",
  "legal.security.disclosure.heading": "Disclosure",
  // `{days}` is a number here, not a phrase, so unlike `{ackWindow}` it carries
  // no English across the boundary and the unit stays translatable.
  "legal.security.disclosure.p1": "Tell us first, and give us {days} days before publishing. If we fix it sooner we will say so and you can publish then.",
  "legal.security.disclosure.p2": "If we go quiet, publish. Silence from us is not a reason for a vulnerability to stay secret.",
  "legal.security.security-txt.heading": "security.txt",
  // `security.txt` and RFC 9116 are the filename and the specification. The
  // field names the file contains (`Contact`, `Expires`, `Preferred-Languages`,
  // `Canonical`, `Policy`) are defined by that RFC, are never translated, and
  // are correctly absent from this catalogue: `securityTxtFields` builds them
  // and a translated field name would produce a file no scanner can parse.
  "legal.security.security-txt.p1": "The machine readable version of this page follows RFC 9116. It gives the contact address, the preferred languages, the canonical location of the file itself, and an expiry date after which it should not be trusted.",
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Twenty-three clauses that had no key at all, added 2026-08-21.
  //
  // A key the catalogue does not state is not an error: the renderer falls back
  // to the English document, which is the same sentence. That is true, and it is
  // why nothing failed. It is also why nobody noticed that twenty-three clauses
  // were falling back in EVERY language, inside documents whose other clauses
  // were translated, so an Italian privacy notice carried an English cookie
  // section under an Italian heading.
  //
  // What was missing is not a random sample. The whole of section 7 (cookies and
  // device storage), the tail of section 4 (the GDPR Art. 6(1)(f) basis, the
  // Art. 21 right to object, the Art. 22 right to human review and the 30-day
  // deadline), the legend that explains how to read the sub-processor list, and
  // both federated sign-in vendors including their United States transfer
  // disclosures. Those are the paragraphs their sections exist to communicate.
  //
  // `legal-parity.test.ts` now refuses a renderable clause that states no key,
  // against an explicit allowlist of the eight that are a name, an address or an
  // email address and must never be translated.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  "legal.privacy.automated.p3": "Password reset stays open at the second-highest level on purpose, because the commonest honest way to reach it is somebody whose password is being guessed at by a stranger, and taking the reset away at that moment locks the real owner out while the attack runs.",
  "legal.privacy.automated.p4": "The basis is our legitimate interest in keeping the service usable and its costs bounded (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)). You can object to it under Art. 21, and you can ask a person to look at any refusal, using the address on this page. We answer within 30 days and it costs nothing.",
  "legal.privacy.automated.p5": "Inside the product, agents propose actions. An action with a legal or similarly significant effect on a person requires a recorded human approval before it can run, and the approval is stored as a scoped, bounded record rather than a flag. That record is the evidence of the human involvement.",
  "legal.privacy.cookies.heading": "Cookies and what is stored on your device",
  "legal.privacy.cookies.p1": "The public website sets no cookie at all. It runs no analytics, no advertising and no third party embed, so there is nothing to ask you about and no banner to click away. A banner offering a choice that does not exist is worse than no banner, so there is not one.",
  "legal.privacy.cookies.p2": "Signing in sets one cookie. It holds a random value that names your session on our side, it is marked so that no script on the page can read it, and it is bound to this one hostname so no other site can set or read it. It lasts ninety days and moves forward while you keep using Orvay, so somebody who visits weekly is never signed out and somebody who walks away is gone ninety days later. Signing out ends it immediately, and you can end any session from your account.",
  "legal.privacy.cookies.p3": "The bot check on the credential forms may also store something on your device while it decides whether you are a person. That is Cloudflare, not us, and it is described in their documentation.",
  "legal.privacy.cookies.p4": "Neither is optional, and neither asks for consent, because both are needed for something you asked for: staying signed in, and being let past the door. That is the exemption Swiss and EU rules both draw for strictly necessary storage. Refusing them is possible and the effect is plain: block cookies for this site in your browser and you can read the public pages but you cannot stay signed in.",
  "legal.privacy.required.p2": "To create an account, yes. An address and a password are required to enter the contract, because they are how the account is reached and how you are told it is you. Without them there is no account to give you. That is the whole of it: nothing else on the sign-in screen is optional data we are collecting while we have your attention.",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair1.term": "In use today",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair2.term": "Wired, and receiving only where its credential is set",
  "legal.subprocessors.how-to-read.pair3.term": "Named in the product, connected to nothing",
  "legal.subprocessors.cloudflare.data6": "Bot check signals on every credential form: your network address, your browser and how it behaves, and a token proving the check was passed. Cloudflare uses these to tell people from scripts, and also to improve that judgement for its own service, which makes it a controller for that purpose rather than only our processor",
  "legal.subprocessors.google.service": "Sign in with Google, when you choose that button rather than a password.",
  "legal.subprocessors.google.data1": "The fact that you asked to sign in, and the address and basic profile Google returns to us afterwards",
  "legal.subprocessors.google.location1": "Google processes in the United States and elsewhere",
  "legal.subprocessors.google.safeguard": "Google is established in the United States. Transfers rest on the standard contractual clauses in its terms. Choosing a password instead means no data reaches Google at all.",
  "legal.subprocessors.google.statusDetail": "Only reached if you press the button. Nothing is sent to Google by opening the sign-in page, and this entry was missing from the list until 20 August 2026 while the button was already on screen.",
  "legal.subprocessors.github.service": "Sign in with GitHub, when you choose that button rather than a password.",
  "legal.subprocessors.github.data1": "The fact that you asked to sign in, and the address and basic profile GitHub returns to us afterwards",
  "legal.subprocessors.github.location1": "GitHub processes in the United States and elsewhere",
  "legal.subprocessors.github.safeguard": "GitHub is established in the United States and owned by Microsoft. Transfers rest on the standard contractual clauses in its terms. Choosing a password instead means no data reaches GitHub at all.",
  "legal.subprocessors.github.statusDetail": "Only reached if you press the button. Nothing is sent to GitHub by opening the sign-in page, and this entry was missing from the list until 20 August 2026 while the button was already on screen."
};

